## ✅ Step 2: Gravitational Force Calculation

Use Newton's law of universal gravitation. We need to compute the gravitational force in 3D as a vector.

### C++ Implementation (code_generator):

```cpp
void calculateGravitationalForce(const CelestialBody& body1, const CelestialBody& body2, Vector3& forceOnBody1, Vector3& forceOnBody2) {
    const float G = 6.67430e-11; // Gravitational constant (m^3 kg^-1 s^-2)

    // Position difference vector from body2 to body1
    Vector3 r = body1.position - body2.position;
    float rSquared = dot(r, r);
    float rMagnitude = sqrt(rSquared);

    // Gravitational force magnitude
    float forceMagnitude = G * body1.mass * body2.mass / rSquared;

    // Normalize direction vector
    Vector3 rDirection = normalize(r);

    // Apply force vector
    forceOnBody1 = forceMagnitude * rDirection;
    forceOnBody2 = -forceMagnitude * rDirection;
}
```

This generates a 3D gravitational force vector for each object. The `Vector3` class must be defined with standard operations for dot product and normalization.

